2FA, device binding, session management; client funds protection.

110
00:08:17.377 --> 00:08:18.273
Part 3.

111
00:08:18.553 --> 00:08:20.068
High-Level Architecture.

112
00:08:21.318 --> 00:08:22.662
Here is the diagram.

113
00:08:26.512 --> 00:08:27.579
Key decisions:

114
00:08:28.929 --> 00:08:30.273
Here is a quick table:

115
00:08:30.553 --> 00:08:32.323
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

116
00:08:32.603 --> 00:08:33.883
Trading core state.

117
00:08:34.163 --> 00:08:34.995
Choice:

118
00:08:35.275 --> 00:08:40.779
In memory (orders, positions, margins per client), with a durable journal and snapshots.

119
00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:41.742
Why:

120
00:08:42.022 --> 00:08:48.422
Microsecond-to-millisecond checks; recover by replay (Ch 55 L2 determinism).

121
00:08:48.702 --> 00:08:49.619
Partitioning.

122
00:08:49.899 --> 00:08:50.731
Choice:

123
00:08:51.011 --> 00:08:56.793
By client ID across RMS or OMS shards; each client's state owned by one shard.

124
00:08:57.073 --> 00:08:57.755
Why:

125
00:08:58.035 --> 00:09:01.619
All of a client's orders are checked against one consistent state.

126
00:09:01.899 --> 00:09:03.371
Exchange connectivity.

127
00:09:03.651 --> 00:09:04.483
Choice:

128
00:09:04.763 --> 00:09:08.646
Dedicated gateways per exchange segment, with limits per connection.

129
00:09:08.926 --> 00:09:09.609
Why:

130
00:09:09.889 --> 00:09:13.153
Exchange rules on order rates per connection (VERIFY).

131
00:09:13.433 --> 00:09:14.435
Market data.

132
00:09:14.715 --> 00:09:15.547
Choice:

133
00:09:15.827 --> 00:09:17.854
Separate ticker plant from trading core.

134
00:09:18.134 --> 00:09:18.817
Why:

135
00:09:19.097 --> 00:09:21.465
Very different load shapes; isolation.

136
00:09:21.745 --> 00:09:22.769
Back office.

137
00:09:23.049 --> 00:09:23.881
Choice:

138
00:09:24.161 --> 00:09:27.233
Batch and event-driven, strongly consistent ledgers.

139
00:09:27.513 --> 00:09:28.195
Why:

140
00:09:28.475 --> 00:09:29.969
Money and regulation.

141
00:09:31.319 --> 00:09:32.236
Deployment.

142
00:09:32.516 --> 00:09:33.348
Choice:

143
00:09:33.628 --> 00:09:41.052
Co-location or proximity hosting for exchange-facing parts; cloud or data centre for client-facing parts.

144
00:09:41.332 --> 00:09:42.015
Why:

145
00:09:42.295 --> 00:09:45.687
Latency to exchange versus elasticity for clients.

146
00:09:47.037 --> 00:09:47.954
Part 4.

147
00:09:48.234 --> 00:09:50.111
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

148
00:09:51.361 --> 00:09:53.260
Journey 1: opening an account.

149
00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:58.831
Basics: the client enters mobile and email (OTP verified) and their PAN.

150
00:09:59.111 --> 00:10:03.484
PAN verification through the tax department's verification service.

151
00:10:03.764 --> 00:10:08.500
KRA check: has this person already done KYC with any intermediary?

152
00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:10.465
If so, fetch those records.

153
00:10:10.745 --> 00:10:14.244
A CKYC lookup may also help (VERIFY current process).

154
00:10:14.524 --> 00:10:15.868
Identity and address:

155
00:10:16.148 --> 00:10:22.249
Aadhaar-based e-KYC or DigiLocker document fetch with consent (VERIFY permitted modes).

156
00:10:22.529 --> 00:10:28.801
A live photo and in-person verification via video or selfie with liveness checks, as required.

157
00:10:30.151 --> 00:10:42.759
Bank verification: a penny drop (1 rupeescredited to verify account holder name via IMPS name lookup) or reverse penny drop (the client pays 1 rupeesvia UPI from their account).

158
00:10:43.039 --> 00:10:47.775
Profile: risk profiling questions, nominee details, segment selection.

159
00:10:48.055 --> 00:10:52.706
F and O activation requires income proof as per current rules (VERIFY).

160
00:10:52.986 --> 00:10:58.213
E-sign: the account opening form is signed with Aadhaar e-sign (OTP-based).

161
00:10:58.493 --> 00:11:09.309
Demat account opened with a depository (CDSL or NSDL) via the broker's DP, and a UCC (unique client code) registered with exchanges.

162
00:11:10.659 --> 00:11:18.104
Activation: the account is ready within hours to a day, once exchange and depository registrations complete.

163
00:11:18.384 --> 00:11:23.077
All documents and verification results are stored encrypted, with audit trails.

164
00:11:23.357 --> 00:11:26.237
KYC data is uploaded to the KRA.

165
00:11:27.587 --> 00:11:29.251
Journey 2: adding funds.

166
00:11:29.531 --> 00:11:39.110
Payment: the client pays via UPI (intent or collect) or net banking through a payment gateway, which calls back on success (Ch 45).

167
00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:48.009
Ledger credit: the funds service credits the client ledger after verifying the payment, and only from the client's own verified bank account.

168
00:11:48.289 --> 00:11:50.955
Third-party funds are not allowed (VERIFY).

169
00:11:51.235 --> 00:11:54.841
Available margin in the RMS increases immediately.

170
00:11:55.121 --> 00:12:01.115
Reconciliation: end-of-day reconciliation matches gateway settlements with ledger credits.

171
00:12:01.395 --> 00:12:02.761
Client money protection:

172
00:12:04.111 --> 00:12:18.703
client funds are held in designated bank accounts;. reported and segregated per exchange and clearing corporation rules;. brokers must not use client funds for other purposes (VERIFY the current segregation and upstreaming rules).

173
00:12:20.053 --> 00:12:22.890
Journey 3: placing a buy order at market open.

174
00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:25.367
It is 9:15:00.

175
00:12:25.647 --> 00:12:32.965
A client places a limit buy for 100 shares of stock X at 500, rupeesproduct CNC (delivery).

176
00:12:33.245 --> 00:12:36.359
Submit: the app sends the order to the API gateway.

177
00:12:36.639 --> 00:12:43.061
The gateway checks: the session and 2FA session state;. rate limits;. that the request is well-formed.

178
00:12:43.341 --> 00:12:46.839
OMS validation: instrument exists and is tradable;.

179
00:12:48.189 --> 00:13:00.861
tick size (price in multiples of 0.05, rupeesfor example);. quantity within freeze limits;. price within the day's price band or circuit limits;. order type allowed for this segment.

180
00:13:01.141 --> 00:13:04.747
It assigns an internal order ID and journals the order.

181
00:13:05.027 --> 00:13:08.227
RMS pre-trade check on the client's shard, in memory:

182
00:13:09.577 --> 00:13:34.878
required margin: for delivery equity buys, full value plus charges (50,000 rupees + estimated charges); for intraday or F and O, margin per rules (§6.1);. available margin = ledger cash + collateral value (after haircut) minus margin blocked by open orders and positions;. if sufficient, block the margin for this order;. if not, reject with a reason.

183
00:13:35.158 --> 00:13:38.891
Route: the OMS sends the order to the exchange gateway, which:

184
00:13:40.241 --> 00:13:51.313
sends it over the exchange's trading interface (binary protocol over leased lines);. respects per-connection message-rate limits;. distributes orders across multiple connections.

185
00:13:51.593 --> 00:13:57.439
Exchange acknowledgement (order accepted into the book, with an exchange order ID) comes back.

186
00:13:57.719 --> 00:14:02.817
The OMS updates the order to OPEN and pushes an update to the client over WebSocket.

187
00:14:03.097 --> 00:14:09.156
Match: when the order matches at the exchange, trade confirmations arrive, possibly partial fills.

188
00:14:10.506 --> 00:14:13.770
The OMS updates the filled quantity and average price.

189
00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:18.573
The RMS converts blocked margin into used margin for the position.

190
00:14:18.853 --> 00:14:20.175
Positions update.

191
00:14:20.455 --> 00:14:21.885
The client sees Complete.

192
00:14:22.165 --> 00:14:25.279
Modify or cancel requests follow the same path.

193
00:14:25.559 --> 00:14:28.226
The RMS re-computes the blocked margin.

194
00:14:28.506 --> 00:14:40.090
Race conditions (a cancel arriving after a fill) are resolved by the exchange's responses, and the OMS state machine handles every order of events (§5.1).

195
00:14:41.440 --> 00:15:00.256
End of day: the trade appears in the contract note (emailed within the regulatory deadline; VERIFY), with brokerage, STT, exchange transaction charges, GST, SEBI fees and stamp duty computed by the back office;. the settlement obligation is computed (§4.5).

196
00:15:01.606 --> 00:15:04.550
Journey 4: an options trader on expiry day.

197
00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:13.278
Option chain: the client watches an option chain: all strikes for an index expiry, with live prices, open interest and Greeks.

198
00:15:13.558 --> 00:15:16.737
The ticker streams the visible strikes only, conflated.

199
00:15:17.017 --> 00:15:27.107
Greeks are computed by a pricing service from live prices, using Black-Scholes-style models with implied volatility solved numerically (§6.6).

200
00:15:27.387 --> 00:15:33.105
Order and margin: the client buys and sells options in a basket (for example, a spread).

201
00:15:33.385 --> 00:15:36.734
The RMS computes the margin for the combined position:

202
00:15:38.084 --> 00:15:45.252
SPAN-style scenario margin (the worst loss across price and volatility scenarios);. plus exposure margin.

203
00:15:45.532 --> 00:15:50.652
Hedged positions need far less margin than each leg alone (§6.1).

204
00:15:50.932 --> 00:15:56.372
Real-time risk: as prices move, M2M losses reduce available margin.

205
00:15:56.652 --> 00:16:02.220
If margin falls below thresholds, the RMS: sends warnings;. blocks new positions;.

206
00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:11.826
auto square-off of intraday positions near the cut-off time, or on margin shortfall (per the broker's policy and rules; VERIFY).

207
00:16:12.106 --> 00:16:15.007
Expiry: positions settle per exchange rules.

208
00:16:15.287 --> 00:16:19.149
Physical settlement applies to stock options in the money (VERIFY).

209
00:16:19.429 --> 00:16:24.250
Obligations are computed by the clearing corporation, and the back office reconciles.

210
00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:26.517
Journey 5:

211
00:16:26.797 --> 00:16:29.357
T+1 settlement of a delivery trade.

212
00:16:29.637 --> 00:16:31.045
The trade is on day T.

213
00:16:31.325 --> 00:16:54.109
Buy side: on T, the clearing corporation computes obligations per broker (net funds and securities per client and settlement type);. on T+1, the broker pays the funds obligation (from client ledgers) and receives securities in its pool account;. the securities are then transferred to the client's demat account (VERIFY the current direct-payout mechanisms).

214
00:16:55.459 --> 00:17:16.089
Sell side (of holdings): the client authorises the sale, via an e-DIS or TPIN or mandate to the depository before selling, or through a pre-authorisation (VERIFY current mechanism);. on T+1, the securities are delivered from the client's demat account;. the funds credit the client's ledger after pay-out.

215
00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:17.307
Failures:

216
00:17:18.657 --> 00:17:28.300
short delivery (the seller didn't deliver) triggers an auction by the exchange, and penalties to the defaulting client;. the back office handles these exceptions.

217
00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:29.753
Settlement cycle:

218
00:17:30.033 --> 00:17:39.079
India moved to T+1 for equities in 2023, and has piloted optional T+0 settlement (VERIFY current status).

219
00:17:40.429 --> 00:17:41.410
Journey 6:

220
00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:43.930
GTT (good till triggered) order.

221
00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:55.943
Setup: the client sets Sell 50 shares of Y if price ≥ 1,200 rupeesor at most 950 rupees (an OCO, one-cancels-other).

222
00:17:56.223 --> 00:18:04.117
Storage: the GTT engine stores both triggers with no margin blocked (it's not yet an order), valid for up to a year.

223
00:18:04.397 --> 00:18:11.970
Watching: on every tick of Y, the engine checks triggers efficiently with price-sorted structures (§6.5).

224
00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:23.749
Trigger: when the last traded price crosses 1,200, rupeesthe engine creates a real order through the OMS, with RMS checks at that moment, and cancels the other leg.

225
00:18:25.099 --> 00:18:27.659
Notification: the client is notified.

226
00:18:27.939 --> 00:18:33.379
If the order is rejected (for example, holdings already sold), the client is told why.

227
00:18:34.729 --> 00:18:35.731
Journey 7:

228
00:18:36.011 --> 00:18:38.550
IPO application and mutual funds.

229
00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:54.147
IPO: the client applies through the broker's app;. the broker submits the bid to the exchange's IPO platform with the client's UPI ID;. the client approves a UPI mandate to block funds in their bank account (ASBA);. on allotment,

230
00:18:54.427 --> 00:19:02.705
the blocked amount is debited and shares credited to the demat account; otherwise the mandate is revoked (VERIFY flow and timelines).

231
00:19:04.055 --> 00:19:18.028
Mutual funds: orders are placed via exchange MF platforms or directly with RTAs;. payment by UPI or mandate for SIPs;. units are credited to the demat account or held in statement form.

232
00:19:18.308 --> 00:19:22.532
A cut-off time rule decides which day's NAV applies (VERIFY).

233
00:19:23.882 --> 00:19:24.842
Part 5.

234
00:19:25.122 --> 00:19:26.658
Core Subsystems in Depth.

235
00:19:27.908 --> 00:19:29.785
OMS: the order state machine.

236
00:19:30.065 --> 00:19:31.409
Here is the diagram.

237
00:19:35.259 --> 00:19:36.966
Every transition is journaled.

238
00:19:37.246 --> 00:19:52.094
Responses from the exchange can arrive in unexpected orders (a trade before the modify acknowledgement), so the state machine must accept any valid sequence and reconcile quantities from the exchange's messages, which are the truth.

239
00:19:52.374 --> 00:19:56.577
Idempotency: client order IDs prevent duplicates from retries.

240
00:19:56.857 --> 00:19:59.779
Exchange order IDs map to internal IDs.

241
00:20:00.059 --> 00:20:04.369
Recovery: on OMS restart, replay the journal to rebuild state;.

242
00:20:05.719 --> 00:20:11.969
then reconcile with the exchange's order book download for the day, catching anything missed during a disconnect.

243
00:20:13.319 --> 00:20:15.901
RMS: pre-trade and real-time risk.

244
00:20:16.181 --> 00:20:28.959
State per client (in memory): cash balance (ledger);. collateral (pledged holdings times price times (1 minus haircut));. margins blocked by open orders;. margins used by positions;.

245
00:20:29.239 --> 00:20:34.530
M2M P and L;. limits (max order value, max quantity, allowed segments).

246
00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:46.376
Pre-trade check (for every order, within microseconds): compute incremental margin;. check limits and price bands;. block the margin atomically with the order acceptance.

247
00:20:46.656 --> 00:20:57.835
The per-client shard processes that client's events in order, like a single-threaded actor, so no race can double-spend margin (Ch 55 L3 for the risk-check model).

248
00:20:58.115 --> 00:20:59.309
Real-time risk:

249
00:21:00.659 --> 00:21:13.993
on every price update of instruments in a client's positions, recompute M2M and margin utilisation (incrementally, only affected clients);. thresholds trigger alerts and restrictions, then square-off.

250
00:21:14.273 --> 00:21:29.163
Scheduled square-off: at the intraday cut-off (for example, ~3:20 pm for equity; VERIFY), open intraday positions are closed by market orders, a large burst the OMS must pace within exchange limits.

251
00:21:29.443 --> 00:21:39.022
Peak margin compliance: margins must be collected upfront, and exchanges check intraday snapshots of margin sufficiency (VERIFY current rules).

252
00:21:39.302 --> 00:21:42.267
This is why RMS checks everything before sending.

253
00:21:43.617 --> 00:21:45.089
Exchange connectivity.

254
00:21:45.369 --> 00:21:51.193
Protocol: exchanges provide trading interfaces (binary protocols) over leased lines.

255
00:21:51.473 --> 00:21:59.153
Many brokers host servers in the exchange co-location facility or nearby for latency (VERIFY each broker's setup).

256
00:21:59.433 --> 00:22:06.153
Limits: each connection has message-rate limits, so the gateway spreads orders across connections and queues bursts.

257
00:22:06.433 --> 00:22:09.015
Resilience: redundant links and connections.

258
00:22:09.295 --> 00:22:14.393
On disconnect: reconnect;. download order and trade status for reconciliation;.

259
00:22:15.743 --> 00:22:18.901
block new orders for affected segments if necessary.

260
00:22:19.181 --> 00:22:24.322
Drop copy (a separate feed of the broker's trades from the exchange) helps reconcile.

261
00:22:25.672 --> 00:22:26.717
The ticker plant.

262
00:22:26.997 --> 00:22:35.808
Feed handlers receive exchange market data: multicast or TCP broadcast feeds of trades, quotes and depth, in binary formats.

263
00:22:36.088 --> 00:22:40.717
They handle sequence gaps (request retransmission, or use recovery feeds).

264
00:22:40.997 --> 00:22:49.019
Normalisation to an internal format: instrument token, last traded price, volume, OHLC, depth levels, open interest.

265
00:22:49.299 --> 00:22:53.587
State: an in-memory latest snapshot per instrument plus a short history.

266
00:22:53.867 --> 00:22:58.539
Fan-out: clients connect over WebSockets and subscribe to instrument tokens;.

267
00:22:59.889 --> 00:23:22.801
WebSocket servers keep a subscription map (token, then set of connections);. each server receives the ticker stream (internal pub or sub, for example, multicast in the data centre or a message bus) and sends updates to subscribed connections;. conflation (§6.4): per connection per instrument, send only the latest state at most every n ms;.

268
00:23:24.151 --> 00:23:24.940
modes:

269
00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:29.337
LTP (last traded price only, smallest), quote, full (with depth).

270
00:23:29.617 --> 00:23:37.745
Zerodha's public Kite Connect API documents LTP or quote or full modes with binary packets (VERIFY).

271
00:23:38.025 --> 00:23:48.137
Candles: a candle service aggregates ticks into 1-minute OHLCV bars in real time (§6.3), and stores them in a time-series store for charts.

272
00:23:48.417 --> 00:23:57.377
Consumers inside the platform: the RMS (prices for M2M), the GTT or alerts engine, the option pricing service.

273
00:23:58.727 --> 00:24:00.263
Positions and P and L.

274
00:24:00.543 --> 00:24:07.711
Positions: from trades, net quantity and average price per instrument per product type (intraday versus overnight).

275
00:24:07.991 --> 00:24:10.317
Realised P and L when positions close.

276
00:24:10.597 --> 00:24:13.519
Unrealised (M2M) from live prices.

277
00:24:13.799 --> 00:24:20.562
Holdings: from depository records plus today's delivery trades (T1 holdings before settlement completes).

278
00:24:20.842 --> 00:24:24.725
Valued at live prices, with day change and overall P and L.

279
00:24:25.005 --> 00:24:39.277
Tax P and L: capital gains by FIFO lot matching of buys and sells (§6.2), with short-term versus long-term classification and grandfathering rules for older holdings (VERIFY current tax rules).

280
00:24:40.627 --> 00:24:41.651
Back office.

281
00:24:41.931 --> 00:25:05.632
Daily batch after market close: trade files from exchanges;. reconcile with the OMS;. compute charges: brokerage, STT or CTT, exchange transaction charges, SEBI turnover fee, stamp duty, GST (VERIFY current rates);. generate contract notes (digitally signed);. post client ledger entries;. compute settlement obligations per clearing corporation file;.

282
00:25:06.982 --> 00:25:08.262
generate reports.

283
00:25:08.542 --> 00:25:17.801
Corporate actions: dividends credited by the depository or RTA;. splits and bonuses adjust holdings quantities and average prices;.

284
00:25:18.081 --> 00:25:24.203
F and O contracts are adjusted by exchange rules;. historical prices are adjusted for charts.

285
00:25:24.483 --> 00:25:29.902
Pledges: client pledge requests go to the depository for approval via OTP.

286
00:25:30.182 --> 00:25:33.211
Approved pledges add collateral value with haircuts.

287
00:25:34.561 --> 00:25:42.839
Running account settlement: periodic return of unused client funds to bank accounts, as required (VERIFY current frequency).

288
00:25:44.189 --> 00:25:45.490
Trading APIs.

289
00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:51.679
REST APIs for orders, positions, holdings and margins, plus WebSocket market data.

290
00:25:51.959 --> 00:25:55.031
Authentication by login flow and access tokens.

291
00:25:55.311 --> 00:26:01.050
Rate limits per API key (orders per second, requests per second), and per-day order caps.

292
00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:12.679
Algorithmic trading by retail clients is subject to SEBI's framework for API access, algo identification and exchange approval (VERIFY current rules and timelines).

293
00:26:14.029 --> 00:26:14.968
Part 6.

294
00:26:15.248 --> 00:26:16.208
Algorithms.

295
00:26:17.458 --> 00:26:18.951
Margin computation.

296
00:26:19.231 --> 00:26:23.263
Equity delivery buy: full value plus charges (cash required).

297
00:26:23.543 --> 00:26:29.858
Equity intraday: a VaR-based margin plus extreme loss margin, applied per exchange rules.

298
00:26:30.138 --> 00:26:33.146
Leverage is limited by regulation (VERIFY).

299
00:26:33.426 --> 00:26:48.594
F and O, SPAN-style: define scenarios: price moves (up and down by fractions of the price scan range) combined with volatility moves, plus extreme moves;. for each scenario, revalue each position (options by a pricing model);.

300
00:26:49.944 --> 00:26:52.291
sum across the portfolio per underlying.

301
00:26:52.571 --> 00:27:06.117
The scanning risk is the worst total loss across scenarios;. add calendar spread charges, subtract net option value where applicable, apply short-option minimums;. add exposure margin as a percentage of notional.

302
00:27:06.397 --> 00:27:17.555
Exchanges publish risk parameter files (scenario arrays) daily, so brokers compute margins consistently with the clearing corporation (VERIFY the current methodology).

303
00:27:18.905 --> 00:27:26.265
Incremental computation: for a new order, compute the margin of the portfolio with the order minus the margin without it.

304
00:27:26.545 --> 00:27:28.806
Hedging orders can reduce margin.

305
00:27:29.086 --> 00:27:34.910
Performance: precompute per-instrument scenario values from the risk parameter file each morning.

306
00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:38.433
Per order, sum arrays for the affected underlying only.

307
00:27:39.783 --> 00:27:41.703
P and L and tax lot matching.

308
00:27:41.983 --> 00:27:46.527
FIFO matching: keep a queue of buy lots (date, quantity, price) per instrument.

309
00:27:46.807 --> 00:27:52.460
Each sell consumes the oldest lots first, producing realised gain lines with holding period.

310
00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:59.609
Weighted average cost for display (average buy price), while tax reports use FIFO (VERIFY tax rules).

311
00:27:59.889 --> 00:28:08.316
Corporate actions adjust lots: a split doubles quantity and halves price, and a bonus adds zero-cost lots per rules (VERIFY).

312
00:28:08.596 --> 00:28:15.060
Intraday versus delivery classification (speculative versus non-speculative income; VERIFY).

313
00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:17.775
Candle aggregation.

314
00:28:18.055 --> 00:28:26.781
Per instrument per interval: open = first trade price, high = max, low = min, close = last, volume = sum.

315
00:28:27.061 --> 00:28:29.983
Real-time: maintain the current candle in memory.

316
00:28:30.263 --> 00:28:35.149
When a tick crosses the interval boundary, emit the completed candle and start the next.

317
00:28:35.429 --> 00:28:41.637
Higher intervals (5 m, 15 m, 1 h, 1 d) are aggregated from 1-minute candles.

318
00:28:41.917 --> 00:28:47.442
Late or corrected ticks: use the exchange's official closing data for daily candles.

319
00:28:47.722 --> 00:28:54.805
Adjustments: historical candles adjusted for splits and bonuses on the fly, or stored with adjustment factors.

320
00:28:56.155 --> 00:28:57.541
Conflation and fan-out.

321
00:28:57.821 --> 00:29:02.024
Per connection, per instrument: keep a dirty flag and the latest snapshot.

322
00:29:02.304 --> 00:29:07.979
On each flush interval, send the latest snapshot of dirty instruments in one batched binary frame.

323
00:29:08.259 --> 00:29:12.675
Result: bandwidth per client is bounded regardless of market activity.

324
00:29:12.955 --> 00:29:16.155
Clients always see the latest value, never a stale queue.

325
00:29:16.435 --> 00:29:23.176
Slow clients: if a socket's send buffer grows, increase that client's flush interval or drop depth updates.

326
00:29:23.456 --> 00:29:25.056
Never block the fan-out loop.

327
00:29:25.336 --> 00:29:26.168
Sharding:

328
00:29:26.448 --> 00:29:29.157
WebSocket servers are sharded by connection.

329
00:29:29.437 --> 00:29:34.600
Each receives the full tick stream (or a subset for its subscriptions) from the ticker plant.

330
00:29:35.950 --> 00:29:38.851
Trigger matching (GTT, alerts, stop-loss).

331
00:29:39.131 --> 00:29:50.033
Per instrument, two sorted structures: trigger when price ≥ X, sorted ascending by X;. trigger when price at most Y, sorted descending by Y.

332
00:29:50.313 --> 00:29:57.523
On a new price p: pop all triggers with X at most p from the first, and Y ≥ p from the second.

333
00:29:57.803 --> 00:30:00.363
That is O(k log n) for k triggered.

334
00:30:00.643 --> 00:30:03.438
Sorted maps or heaps work, as does a skip list.

335
00:30:03.718 --> 00:30:07.195
Gaps: if the price jumps past several triggers, all fire.

336
00:30:07.475 --> 00:30:10.057
Ordering between them follows creation time.

337
00:30:11.407 --> 00:30:15.439
Scale: millions of triggers across instruments, sharded by instrument.

338
00:30:15.719 --> 00:30:17.724
The engine consumes the ticker stream.

339
00:30:18.004 --> 00:30:22.505
Stop-loss orders placed at the exchange are triggered by the exchange itself.

340
00:30:22.785 --> 00:30:25.239
GTT and alerts are broker-side.

341
00:30:26.589 --> 00:30:29.234
Option pricing and implied volatility.

342
00:30:29.514 --> 00:30:37.663
Pricing: a Black-Scholes-style model for European index options (VERIFY which options are European-style in Indian markets).

343
00:30:37.943 --> 00:30:41.634
Greeks (delta, gamma, theta, vega) are computed analytically.

344
00:30:41.914 --> 00:30:52.175
Implied volatility: solve price(σ) = market price for σ by Newton, Raphson (using vega), with bisection fallback when vega is tiny.

345
00:30:52.455 --> 00:30:57.191
Performance: recompute the Greeks for visible strikes on price updates, throttled.

346
00:30:58.541 --> 00:30:59.523
Surveillance.

347
00:30:59.803 --> 00:31:16.891
Patterns: wash trades (same beneficial owner on both sides);. spoofing or layering (large orders placed and cancelled to move prices);. circular trading among related accounts;. unusual trading before announcements;. pump-and-dump activity in illiquid stocks.

348
00:31:18.241 --> 00:31:28.417
Methods: rules on order-to-trade ratios and cancellation patterns, graph analysis of related accounts, and anomaly detection on volume and price impact.

349
00:31:28.697 --> 00:31:34.563
Exchange alerts come to brokers for investigation (VERIFY broker surveillance obligations).

350
00:31:35.913 --> 00:31:38.559
Order throttling and fairness at market open.

351
00:31:38.839 --> 00:31:45.729
Pre-open session orders and AMOs are queued and sent in a controlled stream at the session start, within exchange limits.

352
00:31:46.009 --> 00:31:50.532
Burst smoothing: token buckets per exchange connection (Ch 39).

353
00:31:50.812 --> 00:31:54.673
FIFO across clients, so no client gets systematic priority.

354
