443
00:36:12.119 --> 00:36:13.122
Time series.

444
00:36:13.402 --> 00:36:14.234
Choice:

445
00:36:14.514 --> 00:36:17.543
ClickHouse or QuestDB or TimescaleDB.

446
00:36:17.823 --> 00:36:18.506
Why:

447
00:36:18.786 --> 00:36:20.130
Fast OHLC queries.

448
00:36:20.410 --> 00:36:21.306
Messaging.

449
00:36:21.586 --> 00:36:22.418
Choice:

450
00:36:22.698 --> 00:36:28.053
Kafka; low-latency internal messaging (Aeron or multicast) for the trading core.

451
00:36:28.333 --> 00:36:29.015
Why:

452
00:36:29.295 --> 00:36:31.599
Separate durability and latency needs.

453
00:36:31.879 --> 00:36:32.775
Warehouse.

454
00:36:33.055 --> 00:36:33.887
Choice:

455
00:36:34.167 --> 00:36:36.770
ClickHouse or Trino + object storage.

456
00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:37.733
Why:

457
00:36:38.013 --> 00:36:38.973
Analytics.

458
00:36:39.253 --> 00:36:40.298
Mobile or web.

459
00:36:40.578 --> 00:36:41.410
Choice:

460
00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:45.701
Native or React Native; web SPA with charting libraries.

461
00:36:45.981 --> 00:36:46.663
Why:

462
00:36:46.943 --> 00:36:47.839
Performance.

463
00:36:48.119 --> 00:36:49.165
Infrastructure.

464
00:36:49.445 --> 00:36:50.277
Choice:

465
00:36:50.557 --> 00:36:59.026
Co-lo or on-prem for exchange-facing systems; cloud or DC for client-facing ones; Kubernetes for stateless services.

466
00:36:59.306 --> 00:36:59.989
Why:

467
00:37:00.269 --> 00:37:02.039
Latency and regulation.

468
00:37:02.319 --> 00:37:03.407
Observability.

469
00:37:03.687 --> 00:37:04.519
Choice:

470
00:37:04.799 --> 00:37:09.386
Prometheus, Grafana, OpenTelemetry; latency histograms per hop.

471
00:37:09.666 --> 00:37:10.349
Why:

472
00:37:10.629 --> 00:37:12.186
Market-hours SLOs.

473
00:37:13.536 --> 00:37:14.432
Part 9.

474
00:37:14.712 --> 00:37:17.315
Security, Compliance and Client Protection.

475
00:37:18.565 --> 00:37:30.085
Authentication: mandatory 2FA (TOTP or app-based), device binding, session expiry daily, and alerts on new logins (VERIFY exchange or SEBI requirements).

476
00:37:30.365 --> 00:37:39.069
Client funds: segregation, daily reporting to exchanges and clearing corporations, and restrictions on use (VERIFY current rules).

477
00:37:39.349 --> 00:37:42.293
Securities: held in clients' own demat accounts.

478
00:37:42.573 --> 00:37:49.954
Pledges and sale authorisations go through depository mechanisms with client consent (OTP or TPIN).

479
00:37:51.304 --> 00:37:59.731
KYC or AML: periodic KYC updates, PEP and sanctions screening, and suspicious transaction reporting (VERIFY).

480
00:38:00.011 --> 00:38:01.205
Cybersecurity:

481
00:38:01.485 --> 00:38:10.637
SEBI's cybersecurity and cyber-resilience framework for market intermediaries (audits, SOC, incident reporting; VERIFY the current framework).

482
00:38:10.917 --> 00:38:15.525
Data localisation and DPDP Act for personal data (VERIFY).

483
00:38:16.875 --> 00:38:17.729
Part 10.

484
00:38:18.009 --> 00:38:19.929
Reliability and Operations.

485
00:38:21.179 --> 00:38:32.677
Market-hours discipline: no deployments to trading systems during market hours, and pre-market health checks (connectivity, file loads, risk parameter files, margin calculations).

486
00:38:32.957 --> 00:38:47.400
The 9:15 am spike: logins, WebSocket connects, pre-open and AMO order releases and first trades all at once;. pre-scale WebSocket and API fleets;. stagger AMO releases within exchange limits;.

487
00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:56.942
cache instrument master and static data at clients (downloaded before market);. use backoff with jitter on reconnect (Ch 18).

488
00:38:57.222 --> 00:38:58.139
Failover:

489
00:38:58.419 --> 00:39:08.062
OMS or RMS shards have hot standbys that replay the same journal;. failover within seconds;. reconcile with the exchange after failover.

490
00:39:09.412 --> 00:39:17.753
Exchange outage or disconnection: block new orders for that segment, show clear status, and reconcile when the connection is restored.

491
00:39:18.033 --> 00:39:28.295
Incident reporting: brokers may need to report technical glitches to exchanges within set timelines, with root cause analysis (VERIFY the current framework).

492
00:39:28.575 --> 00:39:32.159
Volatile days: circuit breakers at the exchange halt trading.

493
00:39:32.439 --> 00:39:38.796
Broker systems must handle halts, price band changes and surges in square-offs and margin calls.

494
00:39:40.146 --> 00:39:41.149
Part 11.

495
00:39:41.429 --> 00:39:42.474
Key Trade-offs.

496
00:39:43.724 --> 00:39:45.068
Here is a quick table:

497
00:39:45.348 --> 00:39:47.865
Decision, and Chosen, and Accepted cost.

498
00:39:48.145 --> 00:39:49.148
Trading core.

499
00:39:49.428 --> 00:39:50.260
Chosen:

500
00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:53.548
In-memory, single-writer per client shard, journaled.

501
00:39:53.828 --> 00:39:55.001
Accepted cost:

502
00:39:55.281 --> 00:39:57.543
Complex recovery; careful sharding.

503
00:39:57.823 --> 00:39:58.676
Margin.

504
00:39:58.956 --> 00:39:59.788
Chosen:

505
00:40:00.068 --> 00:40:02.436
Scenario-based, incremental per order.

506
00:40:02.716 --> 00:40:03.889
Accepted cost:

507
00:40:04.169 --> 00:40:06.239
Morning precompute and complexity.

508
00:40:06.519 --> 00:40:07.521
Market data.

509
00:40:07.801 --> 00:40:08.633
Chosen:

510
00:40:08.913 --> 00:40:10.812
Conflated snapshots per client.

511
00:40:11.092 --> 00:40:12.265
Accepted cost:

512
00:40:12.545 --> 00:40:14.209
Clients don't see every tick.

513
00:40:14.489 --> 00:40:15.385
Settlement.

514
00:40:15.665 --> 00:40:16.497
Chosen:

515
00:40:16.777 --> 00:40:19.380
Batch reconciliation with exchange files.

516
00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:20.833
Accepted cost:

517
00:40:21.113 --> 00:40:22.799
End-of-day processing windows.

518
00:40:23.079 --> 00:40:24.039
Location.

519
00:40:24.319 --> 00:40:25.151
Chosen:

520
00:40:25.431 --> 00:40:29.164
Co-location for exchange-facing, cloud for client-facing.

521
00:40:29.444 --> 00:40:30.617
Accepted cost:

522
00:40:30.897 --> 00:40:32.391
Two infrastructure models.

523
00:40:32.671 --> 00:40:33.695
GTT.

524
00:40:33.975 --> 00:40:34.807
Chosen:

525
00:40:35.087 --> 00:40:36.409
Broker-side triggers.

526
00:40:36.689 --> 00:40:37.863
Accepted cost:

527
00:40:38.143 --> 00:40:40.660
Broker must run a reliable trigger engine.

528
00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:42.949
Part 12.

529
00:40:43.229 --> 00:40:44.573
Interview Q and A.

530
00:40:45.823 --> 00:40:46.655
Beginner.

531
00:40:46.935 --> 00:40:47.959
Question 1.

532
00:40:48.239 --> 00:40:51.247
What happens when you place a stock order through a broker app?

533
00:40:51.527 --> 00:40:52.892
Thirty-second answer:

534
00:40:53.172 --> 00:41:16.233
The app sends it to the broker's API; the OMS validates it; the RMS checks and blocks margin; the exchange gateway sends it to the exchange; the exchange acknowledges and later matches it; trade confirmations update the order, positions and margin; the client sees updates over WebSocket; end-of-day processing generates the contract note and settlement.

535
00:41:17.583 --> 00:41:18.586
Question 2.

536
00:41:18.866 --> 00:41:22.834
What is margin, and why does the broker block it before sending an order?

537
00:41:23.114 --> 00:41:24.479
Thirty-second answer:

538
00:41:24.759 --> 00:41:38.967
Margin is the money (or collateral) required to hold a position; the broker blocks it upfront so it never sends an order the client can't cover, as required by exchange and regulatory rules on upfront margin collection.

539
00:41:40.317 --> 00:41:41.363
Question 3.

540
00:41:41.643 --> 00:41:44.245
How do live prices appear on millions of phones?

541
00:41:44.525 --> 00:41:45.891
Thirty-second answer:

542
00:41:46.171 --> 00:42:00.677
The broker receives exchange feeds, normalises them in a ticker plant, and streams updates over WebSockets to clients subscribed to each instrument, conflated so each client gets the latest value at most every fraction of a second.

543
00:42:02.027 --> 00:42:03.094
Question 4.

544
00:42:03.374 --> 00:42:05.934
What does T+1 settlement mean for a buyer?

545
00:42:06.214 --> 00:42:07.579
Thirty-second answer:

546
00:42:07.859 --> 00:42:20.361
Trades done on day T settle on the next working day: the buyer's funds go to the clearing corporation and shares arrive in the buyer's demat account on T+1 via the broker's settlement process.

547
00:42:21.711 --> 00:42:22.713
Intermediate.

548
00:42:22.993 --> 00:42:24.103
Question 5.

549
00:42:24.383 --> 00:42:27.199
Design the order management system's state machine.

550
00:42:27.479 --> 00:42:28.844
Thirty-second answer:

551
00:42:29.124 --> 00:42:50.863
Received, then (rejected pending new), then open, then partially filled, then filled; with pending modify or cancel states; journal every transition; accept exchange messages in any valid order and trust exchange quantities; idempotent client order IDs; recover by journal replay and reconcile with the exchange's order book.

552
00:42:52.213 --> 00:42:53.322
Question 6.

553
00:42:53.602 --> 00:42:56.823
How do you prevent a client from spending the same margin twice?

554
00:42:57.103 --> 00:42:58.469
Thirty-second answer:

555
00:42:58.749 --> 00:43:11.975
Partition RMS by client so one shard owns each client's state and processes their events sequentially; block margin atomically with order acceptance; release or convert on exchange responses.

556
00:43:13.325 --> 00:43:14.413
Question 7.

557
00:43:14.693 --> 00:43:16.741
How are OHLC candles built for charts?

558
00:43:17.021 --> 00:43:18.387
Thirty-second answer:

559
00:43:18.667 --> 00:43:33.984
Aggregate ticks per instrument per interval in memory (first, max, min, last, sum of volume), emit on interval boundaries, store in a time-series database, build higher intervals from 1-minute candles, and adjust history for corporate actions.

560
00:43:35.334 --> 00:43:36.358
Question 8.

561
00:43:36.638 --> 00:43:38.409
How does a GTT order work?

562
00:43:38.689 --> 00:43:40.054
Thirty-second answer:

563
00:43:40.334 --> 00:43:54.649
The broker stores the trigger (no margin blocked), watches every tick with price-sorted trigger structures, and when the condition is met, places a real order through the OMS with RMS checks, cancelling the other leg for OCO.

564
00:43:55.999 --> 00:43:57.065
Question 9.

565
00:43:57.345 --> 00:43:59.265
How are funds added and protected?

566
00:43:59.545 --> 00:44:00.911
Thirty-second answer:

567
00:44:01.191 --> 00:44:18.599
Payment via UPI or net banking from the client's verified bank account, ledger credit after verification, immediate margin update, daily reconciliation; client funds held in designated accounts with segregation and reporting as required by regulations.

568
00:44:19.949 --> 00:44:20.717
Senior.

569
00:44:20.997 --> 00:44:22.042
Question 10.

570
00:44:22.322 --> 00:44:25.394
How would you compute F and O margins in real time?

571
00:44:25.674 --> 00:44:27.039
Thirty-second answer:

572
00:44:27.319 --> 00:44:46.797
Load the exchange's daily risk parameter files, precompute scenario loss arrays per instrument, and for each order compute portfolio margin with and without the order, worst-case scenario loss per underlying plus spread charges, net option value adjustments and exposure margin, so hedges reduce margin.

573
00:44:48.147 --> 00:44:49.277
Question 11.

574
00:44:49.557 --> 00:44:52.907
Design the ticker plant for 3 million concurrent clients.

575
00:44:53.187 --> 00:44:54.552
Thirty-second answer:

576
00:44:54.832 --> 00:45:14.224
Feed handlers with gap recovery, normalisation to compact binary, in-memory latest snapshots, internal pub or sub to a sharded WebSocket fleet with per-connection subscription maps, per-client conflation with a flush interval, modes by detail level, slow-client handling, and pre-scaling for the market open.

577
00:45:15.574 --> 00:45:16.662
Question 12.

578
00:45:16.942 --> 00:45:20.697
How do you recover the OMS after a crash during market hours?

579
00:45:20.977 --> 00:45:22.342
Thirty-second answer:

580
00:45:22.622 --> 00:45:38.601
Hot standby replays the same journal; on failover it resumes from the last journaled state; then reconciles with the exchange's order and trade downloads to catch messages received during the gap; new orders may be paused briefly for affected segments.

581
00:45:39.951 --> 00:45:41.231
Question 13.

582
00:45:41.511 --> 00:45:44.199
How do you compute capital gains for tax reports?

583
00:45:44.479 --> 00:45:45.844
Thirty-second answer:

584
00:45:46.124 --> 00:46:03.169
Per instrument, match sells against buy lots FIFO, producing gain lines with holding periods for short- versus long-term classification, adjusting lots for splits and bonuses, applying grandfathering and current tax rules, and separating intraday (speculative) income.

585
00:46:04.519 --> 00:46:05.714
Question 14.

586
00:46:05.994 --> 00:46:08.298
How do you handle 9:15 am?

587
00:46:08.578 --> 00:46:09.943
Thirty-second answer:

588
00:46:10.223 --> 00:46:26.266
Pre-scale APIs and WebSockets, pre-download static data to clients, stagger AMO releases within exchange connection limits, shard OMS or RMS by client, use backoff with jitter for reconnects, and watch per-hop latency dashboards.

589
00:46:27.616 --> 00:46:28.512
Architect.

590
00:46:28.792 --> 00:46:29.987
Question 15.

591
00:46:30.267 --> 00:46:32.421
Design a broker platform end to end.

592
00:46:32.701 --> 00:46:34.067
Thirty-second answer:

593
00:46:34.347 --> 00:46:55.275
Client apps and APIs behind a gateway; sharded in-memory OMS or RMS with journals; exchange gateways with rate limits and reconciliation; a ticker plant with conflated WebSocket fan-out; GTT and alerts engines on the tick stream; funds and a double-entry client ledger; a back office for contract

594
00:46:55.555 --> 00:47:05.411
notes, obligations and corporate actions; depository integration for holdings and pledges; Kafka to warehouse, surveillance and regulatory reporting.

595
00:47:06.761 --> 00:47:07.955
Question 16.

596
00:47:08.235 --> 00:47:11.393
How do regulations shape the broker's architecture?

597
00:47:11.673 --> 00:47:13.038
Thirty-second answer:

598
00:47:13.318 --> 00:47:33.777
Upfront margin and peak-margin rules force pre-trade RMS; client fund segregation shapes the ledger and banking; T+1 and depository processes shape the back office; KYC rules shape onboarding; algo trading rules shape APIs; cybersecurity and incident reporting frameworks shape operations,

599
00:47:34.057 --> 00:47:39.134
all changing, so rules must be configurable and audited (VERIFY current texts).

600
00:47:40.484 --> 00:47:41.828
Question 17.

601
00:47:42.108 --> 00:47:44.433
How would you run surveillance on client trading?

602
00:47:44.713 --> 00:47:46.079
Thirty-second answer:

603
00:47:46.359 --> 00:48:04.385
Stream orders and trades into a warehouse and streaming rules engine; detect wash trades, spoofing patterns (order-to-trade and cancellation ratios), circular trading via related-account graphs and pre-announcement anomalies; investigate exchange alerts; document outcomes as required.

604
00:48:05.735 --> 00:48:06.909
Question 18.

605
00:48:07.189 --> 00:48:10.026
What would you keep in memory, and what in databases?

606
00:48:10.306 --> 00:48:11.671
Thirty-second answer:

607
00:48:11.951 --> 00:48:29.466
In memory: order and risk state per client shard, latest market snapshots, trigger indexes, all journaled and rebuildable; in databases: order or trade history, ledgers, client master, documents, candles; in the warehouse: everything for analytics and reporting.

608
00:48:30.816 --> 00:48:32.053
Question 19.

609
00:48:32.333 --> 00:48:36.408
How do you keep a broker resilient to exchange connectivity problems?

610
00:48:36.688 --> 00:48:38.053
Thirty-second answer:

611
00:48:38.333 --> 00:48:53.821
Redundant links and connections, per-connection rate limits, automatic reconnect with order or trade download reconciliation, drop copy feeds, clear client status and order blocking for affected segments, and incident reporting per exchange rules.

612
00:48:55.171 --> 00:48:56.281
Question 20.

613
00:48:56.561 --> 00:49:00.209
Compare the broker's systems with the exchange's matching engine.

614
00:49:00.489 --> 00:49:01.854
Thirty-second answer:

615
00:49:02.134 --> 00:49:24.385
The exchange matches orders deterministically for all brokers with microsecond latency; the broker validates and risk-checks its own clients, routes orders, streams data to millions, tracks positions and money, and settles, both need journaled in-memory state and determinism, but the broker's hardest problems are risk, fan-out and back-office correctness.

616
00:49:25.735 --> 00:49:26.780
Part 13.

617
00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:27.828
Summary.

618
00:49:29.078 --> 00:49:31.766
The broker checks, routes, streams and settles.

619
00:49:32.046 --> 00:49:33.411
The exchange matches.

620
00:49:33.691 --> 00:49:39.259
The trading core is in-memory, sharded by client, journaled and reconciled with the exchange.

621
00:49:39.539 --> 00:49:41.609
Every order passes pre-trade risk.

622
00:49:41.889 --> 00:49:46.945
The ticker plant turns exchange feeds into conflated WebSocket streams for millions.

623
00:49:47.225 --> 00:49:49.678
Candles and triggers feed off the same stream.

624
00:49:49.958 --> 00:50:00.795
Money and securities follow strict processes: double-entry ledgers, client fund segregation, T+1 obligations, depository pledges and authorisations.

625
00:50:01.075 --> 00:50:10.483
Algorithms matter: scenario-based margins, FIFO tax lots, conflation, sorted trigger indexes, implied volatility, surveillance patterns.

626
00:50:11.833 --> 00:50:14.009
Regulation is a design input.

627
00:50:14.289 --> 00:50:17.148
Keep rules configurable and everything auditable.

628
00:50:18.498 --> 00:50:19.735
